In .NET 10 (and starting with .NET 9 SR1+), `WindowSoftInputModeAdjust.Resize` was deprecated on Android because the underlying `SOFT_INPUT_ADJUST_RESIZE` flag was removed by Google.

The recommended replacement is to use **keyboard insets** via the new `KeyboardInsets` API or handle it manually with padding. Here's the modern approach:

**Option 1 – Use `android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustResize"` + edge-to-edge insets (preferred)**

In your `Platforms/Android/MainActivity.cs`:

```csharp
[Activity(WindowSoftInputMode = SoftInput.AdjustResize, ...)]
public class MainActivity : MauiAppCompatActivity { }
```

Then in your layout, wrap your input in a container that responds to insets. In .NET 10, enable edge-to-edge and use `KeyboardPadding`:

```xml
<Grid RowDefinitions="*, Auto">
    <CollectionView Grid.Row="0" ... />
    <Entry Grid.Row="1" 
           Margin="{OnPlatform Android='0,0,0,0'}"
           x:Name="MessageEntry" />
</Grid>
```

**Option 2 – Subscribe to keyboard geometry changes (most reliable)**

In `MauiProgram.cs`:

```csharp
builder.ConfigureMauiHandlers(handlers =>
{
#if ANDROID
    Microsoft.Maui.Platform.KeyboardManager.HideKeyboard = false;
#endif
});
```

Then in your page, use the `.NET 10` `SoftKeyboardGeometryChanged` event:

```csharp
#if ANDROID
protected override void OnAppearing()
{
    base.OnAppearing();
    var activity = Platform.CurrentActivity;
    if (activity?.Window != null)
    {
        ViewCompat.SetOnApplyWindowInsetsListener(
            activity.Window.DecorView, 
            new KeyboardInsetsListener(bottomPadding =>
            {
                InputBar.Margin = new Thickness(0, 0, 0, bottomPadding);
            }));
    }
}
#endif
```

With a helper class:

```csharp
#if ANDROID
using AndroidX.Core.View;
class KeyboardInsetsListener : Java.Lang.Object, IOnApplyWindowInsetsListener
{
    private readonly Action<double> _callback;
    public KeyboardInsetsListener(Action<double> callback) => _callback = callback;

    public WindowInsetsCompat OnApplyWindowInsets(Android.Views.View v, WindowInsetsCompat insets)
    {
        var imeInsets = insets.GetInsets(WindowInsetsCompat.Type.Ime());
        var navInsets = insets.GetInsets(WindowInsetsCompat.Type.NavigationBars());
        var bottom = Math.Max(imeInsets.Bottom - navInsets.Bottom, 0);
        _callback(bottom / DeviceDisplay.MainDisplayInfo.Density);
        return ViewCompat.OnApplyWindowInsets(v, insets);
    }
}
#endif
```

**Key takeaway:** The old `AdjustResize` window mode still triggers inset callbacks in .NET 10—it just no longer shrinks the activity automatically. You need to listen for IME insets via `ViewCompat.SetOnApplyWindowInsetsListener` and adjust your layout's bottom margin/padding yourself.
